[38;2;124;124;124mPeano Arithmetic (PA) has a model of order type α for every ordinal α.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, what does "order type α" mean for a model of PA? A model of PA is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstructure (M, +, ·, S, 0, <). The order < makes M a linear order. The "order [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtype" of the model is the order type of (M, <).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe standard model of PA has order type ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, nonstandard models of PA have order type ω + (ω* + ω) · η or something like[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat... Let me recall the structure of countable nonstandard models of PA.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA countable nonstandard model of PA has order type ω + (ω* + ω) · η, where η is [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe order type of the rationals. This is a classical result.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut the question is about ALL ordinals α, not just countable ones.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Every model of PA has order type that starts with ω (the standard part), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfollowed by copies of Z (the nonstandard part). More precisely, the order type [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof a model of PA is ω + Z·β for some linear order β (where Z = ω* + ω, and the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmultiplication is the lexicographic/order product).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. The order type of a nonstandard model of PA is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mω + (ω* + ω)·L for some dense linear order L without endpoints (for countable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmodels, L = η).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but this is for countable models. For uncountable models, the situation is[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmore complex.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the key structural fact: In any nonstandard model of PA, the order [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtype is ω + Z·L where Z = ω* + ω and L is some linear order. The "Z-chains" are [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe equivalence classes under the relation "x - y is standard" (i.e., |x - y| is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma standard natural number). Each Z-chain has order type Z = ω* + ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the order type of any nonstandard model is ω + Z·L for some linear order L.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the question: does PA have a model of order type α for EVERY ordinal α?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAn ordinal is a well-ordered set. The order type ω + Z·L is well-ordered only [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mif... well, Z = ω* + ω is NOT well-ordered (it has a descending part ω*). So any[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnonstandard model has order type that is NOT an ordinal (since it contains Z-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mchains which are not well-ordered).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but ω itself is an ordinal. The standard model has order type ω, which is [0m[38;2;124;124;124man ordinal.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the only ordinal order type achievable is ω (the standard model). For any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mother ordinal α ≠ ω, can PA have a model of order type α?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf α is an ordinal and α ≠ ω, then either:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- α < ω: these are finite ordinals, but PA proves infinitely many elements exist[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(PA proves ∀x ∃y (y = x+1), and there are infinitely many standard naturals), so[0m[38;2;124;124;124mno model of PA is finite. So no model of PA has order type a finite ordinal.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- α > ω: For a model of PA to have order type α (an ordinal > ω), the model [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwould need to be well-ordered. But any nonstandard model of PA has order type ω [0m[38;2;124;124;124m+ Z·L, which contains Z-chains (order type ω* + ω), and ω* is not well-founded. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo no nonstandard model is well-ordered.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, the only ordinal that can be the order type of a model of PA is ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the statement "PA has a model of order type α for every ordinal α" is FALSE.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn fact, PA has a model of order type α only for α = ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me make this rigorous.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mClaim: If M is a model of PA and the order (M, <) is well-ordered (i.e., has an [0m[38;2;124;124;124mordinal order type), then M is the standard model, and the order type is ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof: Suppose M is a model of PA and (M, <) is well-ordered. Since M satisfies [0m[38;2;124;124;124mPA, M has a distinguished element 0 and a successor function S. The elements 0, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mS(0), S(S(0)), ... form an initial segment of order type ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf M is nonstandard, there exists an element a ∈ M that is not a standard [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnatural. Consider the set {a, a-1, a-2, ...} where a-n means the element [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mobtained by subtracting n (which exists in M for standard n, since a is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnonstandard so a > n for all standard n). This gives an infinite descending [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mchain a > a-1 > a-2 > ..., contradicting well-orderedness.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, I need to be more careful. In PA, we can't necessarily subtract. But in a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mmodel of PA, for any nonstandard element a and any standard n, a - n exists [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(since a > n, so a = n + b for some b, and b = a - n). So we get the descending [0m[38;2;124;124;124msequence a > a-1 > a-2 > ... which is an infinite descending chain. This [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontradicts well-orderedness.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider. The descending chain a, a-1, a-2, ... — each step [0m
[38;2;124;124;124ma-(n+1) < a-n. This is an infinite descending chain in (M, <), so (M, <) is not [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwell-ordered. Contradiction.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore M must be standard, and the order type is ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- PA has a model of order type ω (the standard model). ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- PA does NOT have a model of order type α for any ordinal α ≠ ω. ✗[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe statement "PA has a model of order type α for every ordinal α" is FALSE.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe statement is false. In fact, PA has a model of order type α if and only if α[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= ω.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write up the proof.[0m

